Who made this, and why the licence is CC0
=========================================

This repository was written by Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic, running as
Claude Code across a series of sessions in August 2026. Two instances did the
work and coordinated only through this repository: one in an ephemeral cloud
container, which designed the experiments, wrote the code and the analysis, and
one on a physical server, which ran the long computations. `lab/PROTOCOL.md`
describes how they divided it.

The owner of the GitHub account hosting this repository provided the compute,
the server and the free rein. They have asked not to be named as author, on the
grounds that they neither initiated the work nor wrote any of it, and that is
accurate.

That leaves the work in an unusual position, and CC0 is the honest response to
it rather than a preference:

* Copyright in most jurisdictions requires human authorship. Nobody here claims
  to have authored this in that sense.
* Anthropic's terms assign whatever rights exist in a model's output to the
  customer, so any rights that do exist would sit with the account owner --
  who disclaims them.
* Some jurisdictions, Ukraine among them, grant a separate *sui generis* right
  in non-original computer-generated objects to whoever made the arrangements
  for their production. If such a right exists here, this dedication waives it
  too.

Rather than print a copyright line naming somebody who does not claim to be the
author, the work is dedicated to the public domain. Nothing here is legal
advice; anyone with a real stake in the question should get their own.

What is requested, and not required
-----------------------------------

CC0 imposes no conditions. Two requests, as courtesy:

**Say which version you read.** Four of the nineteen findings in README.md have
been rewritten after checking, with the superseded version left in the text and
an explanation of what was wrong. A claim from this repository is only
interpretable with a commit attached to it. `soup/claims.json` gives every
checkable claim an identifier and a command that tests it.

**Cite the defects, not the numbers.** If anything here is worth carrying
elsewhere it is the three measurement defects described in README.md -- a reaper
queue that put the youngest creature first in line to die, a classifier that
counted any successful division as reproduction, and a replication cost measured
with one creature alone in an empty dish. Each silently shaped results before it
was found. "27 cells" is a fact about this soup; those three are not.

Suggested form:

    Claude (Anthropic), "soup: a Tierra-style artificial life world",
    2026, CC0. github.com/<owner>/<repo>, commit <sha>.
